   "source": [
    "# Lab 5\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Matrix Representation: In this lab you will be creating a simple linear algebra system. In memory, we will represent matrices as nested python lists as we have done in lecture. In the exercises below, you are required to explicitly test every feature you implement, demonstrating it works.\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Create a `matrix` class with the following properties:\n",
    "    * It can be initialized in 2 ways:\n",
    "        1. with arguments `n` and `m`, the size of the matrix. A newly instanciated matrix will contain all zeros.\n",
    "        2. with a list of lists of values. Note that since we are using lists of lists to implement matrices, it is possible that not all rows have the same number of columns. Test explicitly that the matrix is properly specified.\n",
    "    * Matrix instances `M` can be indexed with `M[i][j]` and `M[i,j]`.\n",
    "    * Matrix assignment works in 2 ways:\n",
    "        1. If `M_1` and `M_2` are `matrix` instances `M_1=M_2` sets the values of `M_1` to those of `M_2`, if they are the same size. Error otherwise.\n",
    "        2. In example above `M_2` can be a list of lists of correct size.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "2. Add the following methods:\n",
    "    * `shape()`: returns a tuple `(n,m)` of the shape of the matrix.\n",
    "    * `transpose()`: returns a new matrix instance which is the transpose of the matrix.\n",
    "    * `row(n)` and `column(n)`: that return the nth row or column of the matrix M as a new appropriately shaped matrix object.\n",
    "    * `to_list()`: which returns the matrix as a list of lists.\n",
    "    *  `block(n_0,n_1,m_0,m_1)` that returns a smaller matrix located at the n_0 to n_1 columns and m_0 to m_1 rows. \n",
    "    * Modify `__getitem__` implemented above to support slicing.\n",
    "        "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "3. Write functions that create special matrices (note these are standalone functions, not member functions of your `matrix` class):\n",
    "    * `constant(n,m,c)`: returns a `n` by `m` matrix filled with floats of value `c`.\n",
    "    * `zeros(n,m)` and `ones(n,m)`: return `n` by `m` matrices filled with floats of value `0` and `1`, respectively.\n",
    "    * `eye(n)`: returns the n by n identity matrix."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "4. Add the following member functions to your class. Make sure to appropriately test the dimensions of the matrices to make sure the operations are correct.\n",
    "    * `M.scalarmul(c)`: a matrix that is scalar product $cM$, where every element of $M$ is multiplied by $c$.\n",
    "    * `M.add(N)`: adds two matrices $M$ and $N$. Don’t forget to test that the sizes of the matrices are compatible for this and all other operations.\n",
    "    * `M.sub(N)`: subtracts two matrices $M$ and $N$.\n",
    "    * `M.mat_mult(N)`: returns a matrix that is the matrix product of two matrices $M$ and $N$.\n",
    "    * `M.element_mult(N)`: returns a matrix that is the element-wise product of two matrices $M$ and $N$.\n",
    "    * `M.equals(N)`: returns true/false if $M==N$."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "5. Overload python operators to appropriately use your functions in 4 and allow expressions like:\n",
    "    * 2*M\n",
    "    * M*2\n",
    "    * M+N\n",
    "    * M-N\n",
    "    * M*N\n",
    "    * M==N\n",
    "    * M=N\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "6. Demonstrate the basic properties of matrices with your matrix class by creating two 2 by 2 example matrices using your Matrix class and illustrating the following:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "(AB)C=A(BC)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "$$\n",
    "A(B+C)=AB+AC\n",
    "$$\n",
    "$$\n",
    "AB\\neq BA\n",
    "$$\n",
    "$$\n",
    "AI=A\n",
    "$$"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "1. Associativity (AB)C == A(BC): True\n",
      "2. Distributivity A(B+C) == AB + AC: True\n",
      "3. Non-Commutativity AB != BA: True\n",
      "4. Identity AI == A: True\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "class Matrix:\n",
    "    def __init__(self, *args):\n",
    "        # The lab says we need to handle initialization in two ways\n",
    "        \n",
    "        # handling by dimensions Matrix(rows, cols)\n",
    "        if len(args) == 2:\n",
    "            self.n, self.m = args[0], args[1]\n",
    "            # here filling the grid with 0.0 floats\n",
    "            self.data = [[0.0 for _ in range(self.m)] for _ in range(self.n)]\n",
    "        \n",
    "        # handling initialization by a list of lists Matrix([[1,2],[3,4]])\n",
    "        elif len(args) == 1 and isinstance(args[0], list):\n",
    "            input_data = args[0]\n",
    "            self.n = len(input_data)\n",
    "            self.m = len(input_data[0]) if self.n > 0 else 0\n",
    "            \n",
    "            # We need to check if the matrix is properly specified now\n",
    "            # This means that checking that every row has the exact same number of columns.\n",
    "            for row in input_data:\n",
    "                if len(row) != self.m:\n",
    "                    raise ValueError(\"It's a ragged matrix. All rows must be the same length.\")\n",
    "            \n",
    "            # now making a copy so we don't accidentally modify the original list passed in\n",
    "            self.data = [list(row) for row in input_data]\n",
    "\n",
    "    def shape(self):\n",
    "        return (self.n, self.m)\n",
    "\n",
    "    def to_list(self):\n",
    "        return self.data\n",
    "\n",
    "    def __getitem__(self, key):\n",
    "        # supporting now both M[i][j] and M[i, j]\n",
    "        if isinstance(key, tuple):\n",
    "            r_idx, c_idx = key\n",
    "            \n",
    "            # Slicing support\n",
    "            # This checks if sometimes the user did something like M[0:2, :]\n",
    "            if isinstance(r_idx, slice) or isinstance(c_idx, slice):\n",
    "                # We need to slice the rows first\n",
    "                if isinstance(r_idx, slice):\n",
    "                    selected_rows = self.data[r_idx]\n",
    "                else:\n",
    "                    selected_rows = [self.data[r_idx]]\n",
    "                \n",
    "                #  slicing the columns for each of those rows\n",
    "                new_grid = []\n",
    "                for row in selected_rows:\n",
    "                    if isinstance(c_idx, slice):\n",
    "                        new_grid.append(row[c_idx])\n",
    "                    else:\n",
    "                        new_grid.append([row[c_idx]])\n",
    "                return Matrix(new_grid)\n",
    "            \n",
    "            return self.data[r_idx][c_idx]\n",
    "        \n",
    "        # Fallback for M[i] which allows the standard M[i][j] to work\n",
    "        return self.data[key]\n",
    "\n",
    "    def __setitem__(self, key, value):\n",
    "        # Supporting M[i, j] = value\n",
    "        if isinstance(key, tuple):\n",
    "            r, c = key\n",
    "            self.data[r][c] = value\n",
    "        else:\n",
    "            # M1 = M2 (or M1 = list_of_lists)\n",
    "            # We have to check the size matches before assigning\n",
    "            if len(value) != self.m:\n",
    "                raise ValueError(\"Size mismatch for assignment!\")\n",
    "            self.data[key] = list(value)\n",
    "\n",
    "    def transpose(self):\n",
    "        # it flips rows and columns\n",
    "        trans_data = [[self.data[r][c] for r in range(self.n)] for c in range(self.m)]\n",
    "        return Matrix(trans_data)\n",
    "\n",
    "    def row(self, n):\n",
    "        # Returning row as a new 1xM matrix\n",
    "        return Matrix([list(self.data[n])])\n",
    "\n",
    "    def column(self, n):\n",
    "        # Returning column as a new Nx1 matrix\n",
    "        col_list = [[self.data[r][n]] for r in range(self.n)]\n",
    "        return Matrix(col_list)\n",
    "\n",
    "    def block(self, n0, n1, m0, m1):\n",
    "        # it gets a sub-section of the matrix from n0:n1 and m0:m1\n",
    "        # Adding +1 below because these indices are usually inclusive I got to know from gemini the logic here.\n",
    "        sub = [row[m0:m1+1] for row in self.data[n0:n1+1]]\n",
    "        return Matrix(sub)\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "    def scalarmul(self, c):\n",
    "        res = [[val * float(c) for val in row] for row in self.data]\n",
    "        return Matrix(res)\n",
    "\n",
    "    def add(self, N):\n",
    "        if self.shape() != N.shape():\n",
    "            raise ValueError(\"Shapes must match for addition.\")\n",
    "        res = [[self.data[i][j] + N[i,j] for j in range(self.m)] for i in range(self.n)]\n",
    "        return Matrix(res)\n",
    "\n",
    "    def sub(self, N):\n",
    "        if self.shape() != N.shape():\n",
    "            raise ValueError(\"Shapes must match for subtraction.\")\n",
    "        res = [[self.data[i][j] - N[i,j] for j in range(self.m)] for i in range(self.n)]\n",
    "        return Matrix(res)\n",
    "\n",
    "    def mat_mult(self, N):\n",
    "        # the inner dimensions must match (Cols of A == Rows of B)\n",
    "        if self.m != N.shape()[0]:\n",
    "            raise ValueError(\"Inner dimensions mismatch for matrix multiplication.\")\n",
    "        \n",
    "        new_n, new_p = self.n, N.shape()[1]\n",
    "        res_grid = [[0.0 for _ in range(new_p)] for _ in range(new_n)]\n",
    "        \n",
    "        for i in range(new_n):\n",
    "            for j in range(new_p):\n",
    "                # Calculating dot product here manually. I learnt other ways to calculate to with the help og gemini.\n",
    "                total = 0\n",
    "                for k in range(self.m):\n",
    "                    total += self.data[i][k] * N[k, j]\n",
    "                res_grid[i][j] = total\n",
    "        return Matrix(res_grid)\n",
    "\n",
    "    def element_mult(self, N):\n",
    "        if self.shape() != N.shape():\n",
    "            raise ValueError(\"Shapes must match for element-wise mult.\")\n",
    "        res = [[self.data[i][j] * N[i,j] for j in range(self.m)] for i in range(self.n)]\n",
    "        return Matrix(res)\n",
    "\n",
    "    def equals(self, N):\n",
    "        if self.shape() != N.shape(): return False\n",
    "        for i in range(self.n):\n",
    "            for j in range(self.m):\n",
    "                if self.data[i][j] != N[i,j]: return False\n",
    "        return True\n",
    "\n",
    "#overloading the operators\n",
    "\n",
    "    def __add__(self, other): return self.add(other)\n",
    "    def __sub__(self, other): return self.sub(other)\n",
    "    def __mul__(self, other):\n",
    "        # here this checks if we are multiplying by a number or another Matrix\n",
    "        if isinstance(other, (int, float)): return self.scalarmul(other)\n",
    "        return self.mat_mult(other)\n",
    "    def __rmul__(self, other): return self.scalarmul(other) # For 2 * M\n",
    "    def __eq__(self, other): return self.equals(other)\n",
    "\n",
    "# The below function is the standalone functions as directed in the instructions above for the lab.\n",
    "\n",
    "def constant(n, m, c):\n",
    "    return Matrix([[float(c) for _ in range(m)] for _ in range(n)])\n",
    "\n",
    "def zeros(n, m): return constant(n, m, 0)\n",
    "def ones(n, m): return constant(n, m, 1)\n",
    "\n",
    "def eye(n):\n",
    "    # in the identity matrix we have 1.0 on the diagonal, 0.0 everywhere else\n",
    "    data = [[1.0 if i == j else 0.0 for j in range(n)] for i in range(n)]\n",
    "    return Matrix(data)\n",
    "\n",
    "# finally demonstrating Properties as said in the last instructions 6\n",
    "\n",
    "A = Matrix([[1, 2], [3, 4]])\n",
    "B = Matrix([[5, 6], [7, 8]])\n",
    "C = Matrix([[1, 0], [0, 1]]) # Using Identity as C for simplicity\n",
    "I = eye(2)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"1. Associativity (AB)C == A(BC):\", (A * B) * C == A * (B * C))\n",
    "print(\"2. Distributivity A(B+C) == AB + AC:\", A * (B + C) == (A * B) + (A * C))\n",
    "print(\"3. Non-Commutativity AB != BA:\", not (A * B == B * A))\n",
    "print(\"4. Identity AI == A:\", A * I == A)"
   ]
  },
